  "title": "Chapter 7 – Pharmacology",
  "order": 7,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "ft1fyyd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Pharmacokinetics describes the body's response to drugs through Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, and Excretion (ADME).",
        "Drugs originate from diverse natural and synthetic sources and are chosen based on therapeutic requirements.",
        "Routes of drug administration dictate the speed of onset, bioavailability, and targeted tissue action.",
        "Key pharmacological classes include Antibiotics, Analgesics, Antipyretics, Cardiovascular, and Endocrine agents.",
        "Vaccines and immunological drugs provide essential primary prevention and therapeutic immunomodulation."
      ],
      "id": "5mxp3n9"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
